Step 1: Add env file to .gitignore

Open or create .gitignore in the project root:

notepad .gitignore


Add the following lines:

# Environment files
.env
.env.*
envs/.env.local
config/credentials/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# Django
staticfiles/
media/

# Env files
.env
env/
venv/

# Gunicorn
gunicorn.ctl

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log


✅ This ensures env files are never tracked by Git.

✅ Step 2: If .env was already committed (IMPORTANT)

If the env file was accidentally pushed before:

2.1 Remove it from Git tracking (without deleting locally)
git rm --cached envs/.env.local
git commit -m "Remove env file from tracking"
git push


✅ Local file stays
✅ Git stops tracking it

2.2 Permanently remove it from Git history

Required if secrets were already pushed

pip install git-filter-repo
cd kumbha_Backend
git filter-repo --path envs/.env.local --invert-paths
git push origin --force --all
git push origin --force --tags


✅ Removes env file from all past commits

🚨 Step 3: Rotate Secrets

If .env was ever pushed publicly:

Change database passwords

Regenerate API keys

Regenerate Django SECRET_KEY

Update credentials in dashboards (Twilio, AWS, etc.)

Never reuse leaked secrets.

✅ Step 4: Use .env.example (Best Practice)

Create a safe sample file:

envs/.env.example


Example content:

DEBUG=
SECRET_KEY=
RDS_HOST=
RDS_PORT=
RDS_DATABASE=
RDS_USERNAME=
RDS_PASSWORD=
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=


✅ No real values
✅ Safe to commit

Commit it:

git add envs/.env.example
git commit -m "Add env example file"
git push

✅ Step 5: Local setup instructions (for developers)

Each developer should:

cp envs/.env.example envs/.env.local


Then fill in their own values locally.

✅ Final Checklist

✅ .env.local added to .gitignore
✅ .env.local removed from Git history
✅ Secrets rotated
✅ .env.example committed
✅ Safe for public repositories

✅ Common Mistakes to Avoid

❌ Committing .env files
❌ Storing secrets in settings.py directly
❌ Sharing env files over GitHub
❌ Reusing leaked credentials
